md`# SoundScript

SoundScript is TypeScript with some constructs removed. You use it to write notebook cells. A checker reads each cell. It accepts the cell only if the parameter list names everything that the cell depends on. The notebook then knows what can change the value of the cell. It runs the cell again only when an input is computed again. It can run the cell on another thread. It can replay the cell in a test.

This page is the tutorial. The [specification](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-spec")}) defines the language. The S-numbers in this tutorial refer to the specification. Every example is a live cell of this notebook, except the boxes of mistakes in sections 2 and 8. To change an example, open its editor, change the code, and press Shift-Enter. The first example, \`width\`, is under the next heading.`
// %%
md`## A first cell

\`width\`, \`height\` and \`area\` are three cells. A cell is a function. Its parameter names are the cells that it reads. Thus \`area\` runs again when \`width\` or \`height\` changes.

The listing before the cells is their source. The notebook reads the source from the cells as they are now. When edit mode is on, the value of each cell follows the listing. The **edit** bar under a cell opens its editor.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "width", to: "area" })
// %%
//ss: 01-cells.ts -aspect -scale -half -test_half
// %%
md`**Try it.** Open \`width\`, change it to \`() => 800\`, and press Shift-Enter. \`area\`, \`aspect\` and \`half\` update. \`test_half\` fails, because it checks that \`half\` is 128000. Now change \`width\` to \`() => "800"\`. The notebook stores the edit. The editor tells you which cells the edit breaks. \`area\` and \`aspect\` do arithmetic on \`width\`, so the checker rejects them. They show the message from the checker and stop. \`half\` reads \`area\`, so it is blocked. It shows the error from \`area\` and does not run until \`area\` runs. Change \`width\` back, and the cells run again. To keep the string, make two edits in each of the two cells. Change the parameter to \`width: string\`. Change the arithmetic to use \`Number(width)\`.`
// %%
md`## Why take things out

An ordinary notebook cell can do anything that JavaScript can do. It can read \`document\`, call \`Math.random\`, or change an object that another cell returned. The runtime cannot see these things. Thus the value of a cell can depend on more than its inputs. The runtime cannot move the cell to another thread or replay it in a test. A value that another cell returned can change while the cell uses it.

This is common in the lopecode notebooks. A count on 2026-11-08 found 8,650 cells in 279 modules. Of these cells, 941 read a browser global that is not one of their inputs. \`document\` appears in 347 cells and \`window\` in 291. The count used one declared canonical copy of each module from the lopecode and lopebooks notebook collections. It parsed each cell and compared its free names with its inputs.

SoundScript removes the constructs that hide a dependency or an effect. The remaining language is TypeScript, and an editor highlights it as TypeScript.`
// %%
md`## The four rules

1. **Inputs are parameters.** A cell body can use its parameters, its own locals and a fixed list of built-ins. There are no globals.
2. **Effects are parameters.** The clock, randomness, the network and logging are *capabilities*. A capability is a reserved parameter name (\`now\`, \`random\`, \`fetch\`, \`log\`) that the notebook supplies. If a cell can give two answers for the same inputs, its signature shows that.
3. **Values do not change after a cell returns them.** A cell can build a value with local writes. After the cell returns the value, the value is read-only everywhere.
4. **State changes only in actions.** \`state(...)\` makes a state cell. A write to a state cell must be inside \`action(...)\`. A cell never calls an action. The page calls the action, for example when the user clicks a button or when a frame arrives.

Sections 1 to 5 teach these rules. Sections 6 to 10 show what the rules make possible.`
// %%
md`## 1. Cells and their inputs

These are the other cells of the file that contains \`width\`, \`height\` and \`area\`. A parameter can omit its type. It then has the type of the cell that it names. Write a cell whose value is a function as two arrows, as in \`scale\`. A cell named \`test_…\` is a test. The test passes if it does not throw.

The diagram after the cells shows which cells each cell reads.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "aspect", to: "test_half" })
// %%
//ss: 01-cells.ts -width -height -area
// %%
depGraph(tutorial_module, invalidation, { only: ["width", "height", "area", "aspect", "scale", "half", "test_half"] })
