md`## 2. What the checker rejects

The box contains eight cells. Four cells each break one rule. \`moved\` writes an object that it made, which is allowed since 2026-11-22 (S4.1). \`width\` and \`label\` are the cells that the other cells read. \`jittered\` is the correct form of \`jitter\`. The checker checks the cells as you type. The notebook does not run them. Each message starts with the name of the rule in brackets.`
// %%
viewof src_errors = sourceBox(examples.errors)
// %%
checkBox(src_errors)
// %%
md`\`jittered\` is the correct form of \`jitter\`. It takes \`random\` as a parameter. Thus its signature shows that the cell can differ between two runs.

**Try it.** In the box, change \`label: number\` to \`label: string\` in \`doubled\`. The message changes from an input type error to a type error on \`label * 2\`.`
// %%
md`## 3. Local writes

A cell often builds an array or a typed array one element at a time. This is fast, and rule 3 allows it. A value that a cell makes (a literal, \`new Map()\`, a copy) is writable inside that cell, under any number of names. The value that a cell returns is read-only to every cell that takes it. The checker rejects a write to such a value. Where the checker cannot prove that no later write occurs, the value is frozen when it leaves the cell, and a later write throws.

\`grown\` writes an array that it made and returns the array. \`twoNames\` holds one array under two names, and a write through one name shows through the other. The checker accepts both cells.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "samples", to: "twoNames" })
// %%
//ss: 03-mut.ts
// %%
md`**Try it.** Open \`twoNames\` and change \`const done = xs;\` to \`const done = [...xs];\`. Now \`done\` is a copy, and the value of the cell is \`[1, 2, 3]\`.`
// %%
md`## 4. State and views

A state cell is a cell that returns \`state(initial)\`. Other cells use it in two ways. A parameter named \`count\` gets the current value, and the cell runs again when the value changes. A parameter typed \`State<number>\` gets a handle to the state cell. The handle is not a dependency: the cell does not run again when the value changes.

A write must be inside \`action(...)\`. An action reads state with \`read(count)\`, not through a value parameter. Thus it always sees the current value. \`increment\`, \`clear\` and \`save\` are actions. A cell cannot call them. The page calls them when the user clicks a button.

The view is also a cell. \`app\` returns JSX, which is a description of the page. The notebook builds the description again when one of the inputs of \`app\` changes (here \`count\` and \`saved\`). An event attribute such as \`onClick\` must get an action.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "count", to: "app" })
// %%
//ss: 04-counter.tsx
// %%
md`**Try it.** Press **add one**. Then open \`app\` and change \`onClick={increment}\` to \`onClick={count}\`. The checker rejects \`app\`, because a number is not an action. The message replaces the buttons. Change the attribute back. The buttons return, and the count is the same as before.`
// %%
md`An action is synchronous. It runs from start to finish when the page calls it. Its writes commit together, so a cell never sees only some of them. An action cannot \`await\`. To use an answer from a worker or a server, an async cell waits for the answer. A listener then gives the answer to an action, and the action stores it. A write after the action returns, from a callback that the action made, throws.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "measure", to: "measureView" })
// %%
//ss: ./tutorial-async.tsx
// %%
test_async_click = {
  tests_on;
  // a copy whose source answers when this test opens the gate
  const copy = await ss_instance(tutorial_module, invalidation);
  let open;
  const gate = new Promise((r) => (open = r));
  copy._scope.get("measure").define("measure", [], () => (w) => gate.then(() => w.length));
  const view = soundscript.mount(await copy.value("measureView"), null);
  // the listener is observed, so the events cell is pulled
  for (const n of ["onSize", "wordSize"]) await copy.value(n);
  // waits are counted in animation frames until a condition holds; the cap only turns a hang into an error
  const frames = async (f, n = 600) => { for (let i = 0; i < n && !(await f()); i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r)); return f(); };
  try {
    view.querySelector("button").click();
    const asked = await frames(async () => (await copy.value("measureAsked")) === 1);
    const during = await copy.value("wordSize");
    open();
    const done = await frames(async () => (await copy.value("wordSize")) === 11);
    if (!asked || during !== 0) throw new Error(`after the click: asked ${asked}, wordSize ${during}`);
    if (!done) throw new Error(`wordSize is ${await copy.value("wordSize")} after the answer`);
    return "the click asked; measured waited for the answer with wordSize 0; onSize gave 11 to storeSize";
  } finally { open(); }
}
// %%
md`## 5. One function for every change

When state has several fields, separate actions that each change one field can make the state inconsistent. Here every action is the same line, \`store.update(s => step(s, event))\`. Thus one function, \`step\`, decides every transition. \`step\` is a plain function, and \`test_undo\` tests it without a page.`
// %%
reducer_view = framed(soundscript.mount(reducer_app, this))
// %%
sourceListing(reducer_module, invalidation)
// %%
moduleTable(reducer_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-reducer](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-reducer")}).`
// %%
md`**Try it.** Open the reducer module from the link under the table. In \`step\`, change \`- last\` to \`+ last\`. The checker accepts the edit, because the types still agree. \`test_undo\` fails: its row in the table reads \`throws: undo left 6\`. The checker proves that a cell depends only on its inputs. It does not prove that the cell is correct.`
// %%
md`## 6. Running only the cells a change reaches

A cell runs again when one of its inputs was computed again, even if the new value is equal (S8.4). A cell that depends only on its inputs keeps its value and does not run when no input was computed again. The table counts how many times each cell body has run.`
// %%
model_view = framed(soundscript.mount(model_app, this))
// %%
sourceListing(model_module, invalidation)
// %%
variableTable(model_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-model](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-model")}).`
// %%
md`**Try it.** Press **bump** and read the **runs** column. \`doubled\` runs again, because \`count\` changed. \`scale\` runs again, and so do the cells that read \`scale\` (\`area\` and \`areaFn\`). \`areaOf4\` runs again, because it reads \`areaFn\`. \`app\`, the view, runs again because it shows \`count\`. \`side\` and \`bump\` keep their old count, because they do not read \`count\`. \`stamped\` runs again, because it reads \`doubled\`. The **inputs** of \`stamped\` also include \`ss_now\`, the clock. The clock makes the cell not repeatable. It does not make the cell run more often.`
// %%
md`## 7. Running cells on other threads

A cell that uses only its inputs can run on any thread. \`compile\` takes a group of cells and returns an ordinary async function. With a worker pool, the function runs the cells off the main thread. The checker works out the type of the function from the cells that it names.

A pool of workers computes this Mandelbrot set in bands. Move the slider to change the iteration count.`
// %%
workers_view = framed(soundscript.mount(workers_app, this))
// %%
workers_picture = {
  const canvas = this ?? htl.html`<canvas class="lite-mandel">`;
  canvas.width = workers_width;
  canvas.height = workers_height;
  const data = new ImageData(workers_width, workers_height);
  data.data.set(workers_image);
  canvas.getContext("2d").putImageData(data, 0, 0);
  return canvas;
}
// %%
workers_redraw = {
  // workers_maxIterAt runs when maxIter changes, before image is computed, so this is slider to picture.
  // At boot the time includes loading the page, so none is shown.
  workers_picture;
  const ms = (performance.now() - workers_maxIterAt.at).toFixed(0);
  return htl.html`<div class="lite-readout">${workers_maxIter} iterations, ${workers_width}×${workers_height}: ${workers_maxIterAt.boot ? "move the slider to time a redraw" : `redrawn ${ms} ms after the slider moved (${workers_bands.length} bands in ${workers_poolSize} workers, then drawn on the canvas)`}</div>`;
}
// %%
workers_maxIterAt = (workers_maxIter, { at: performance.now(), boot: this === undefined })   // hidden by the style cell
// %%
md`**Try it.** Press **measure again**. The table times the same cells on the main thread and in the pool. It also checks that both give the same bytes.`
// %%
viewof remeasure = Inputs.button("measure again")
// %%
benchmarkView = htl.html`<table class="lite-panel lite-bench">
<tr><td>workers in the pool</td><td>${benchmark.poolSize} (navigator.hardwareConcurrency ${benchmark.hardware})</td></tr>
<tr><td>bands (calls) per image</td><td>${benchmark.bands}</td></tr>
<tr><td>main thread, median of ${benchmark.runs}</td><td>${benchmark.mainMs.toFixed(0)} ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>worker pool, median of ${benchmark.runs}</td><td>${benchmark.poolMs.toFixed(0)} ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>same bytes from both</td><td>${badge(benchmark.same ? "ok" : "rejected", String(benchmark.same))}</td></tr>
</table>`
// %%
sourceListing(workers_module, invalidation)
// %%
moduleTable(workers_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-mandelbrot](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-mandelbrot")}).`
// %%
md`## 8. Mistakes in a view

The checker checks JSX as it checks the rest of a cell. It checks each attribute against the attributes that the tag declares. It checks each event handler against the action that the handler gets. There are eleven cells, each with one mistake. Ten are live cells that do not run. Each shows the message from the checker, and you can correct the cell in place. The eleventh is in a box after them.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "n", to: "ok_all" })
// %%
//ss: 08-reject-jsx.tsx -bad_free_component
// %%
md`\`bad_free_component\` uses a component that does not exist. A live cell would name a variable that the module does not define. Thus the checker checks this cell alone in a box:`
// %%
viewof src_free_component = sourceBox(examples.rejectJsx.split("\n").find((l) => l.includes("bad_free_component")))
// %%
checkBox(src_free_component, { tsx: true })
// %%
md`## 9. A game

This is Breakout. It uses the parts from the earlier sections. \`step(world, event)\` is the full game, as in section 5. The world is one state cell. The view is JSX, in several cells that each read one part of the world. Every view cell runs again on each frame, because \`world\` was computed again. The renderer then keeps the nodes of the page that did not change ("The renderer").

One part is new. A game needs a clock, and a cell cannot call an action. \`ticks\` is a generator cell. While the game plays, it yields the time since the last frame. It yields nothing when \`playing\` is false or the game is over. \`onTick\` returns \`on(ticks, tick)\`, which is a *listener*. The notebook runs the action \`tick\` one time for each value of \`ticks\`. A parameter typed \`Events<number>\` is a handle to the generator, not its latest value. Thus \`onTick\` does not run again on every frame.`
// %%
breakout_view = {
  // the game's own view, with its state (press play, paused, game over) drawn over the board
  const root = this ?? htl.html`<div class="lite-game"><div class="lite-game-state" aria-live="polite"></div></div>`;
  const game = soundscript.mount(breakout_app, root.__game ?? null);
  if (game !== root.__game) { root.__game?.remove(); root.prepend(game); root.__game = game; }
  const label = breakout_over ? (/game over/.test(breakout_status) ? "game over · press new game" : "cleared · press new game") : breakout_playing ? "" : "press play";
  const state = root.querySelector(".lite-game-state");
  if (state.textContent !== label) { state.textContent = label; state.hidden = !label; }
  // the 320×240 field is drawn at up to twice its size, to the width of the column (the CSS scales it
  // by --k; pointer offsets are in the field's own coordinates, so aiming is unchanged)
  if (!root.__resize) {
    root.__resize = new ResizeObserver(() => {
      const w = root.parentElement?.clientWidth ?? 320;
      root.style.setProperty("--k", String(Math.max(0.5, Math.min(2, w / 320))));
    });
    // the root is placed in the page after this cell returns; observe its container once it is there
    const attach = (tries) => root.parentElement ? root.__resize.observe(root.parentElement) : tries && requestAnimationFrame(() => attach(tries - 1));
    attach(600);
    // this cell runs again on every frame of play and reuses root; stop observing only once root has left the page
    invalidation.then(() => queueMicrotask(() => { if (!root.isConnected) root.__resize.disconnect(); }));
  }
  return root;
}
// %%
depGraph(breakout_module, invalidation)
// %%
sourceListing(breakout_module, invalidation)
// %%
moduleTable(breakout_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-breakout](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-breakout")}).`
// %%
md`**Try it.** Press **play** and move the pointer over the board. Then open the breakout module from the link under the table. In \`step\`, change \`< 28\` to \`< 60\`. This number is the largest distance from the centre of the paddle at which the ball bounces. The game keeps its score and its bricks, and the ball continues to move. This is because the world is state, and \`step\` is only the rule that changes it.`
// %%
md`\`test_replay\` plays 600 frames two times, with no browser and no clock. It checks that both runs end with the same score and the same ball position. A recorded list of events always replays to the same game, because \`step\` depends only on its arguments.`
// %%
md`## 10. Working with ordinary cells

A SoundScript cell can read the other cells of its module: Observable JavaScript cells, raw cells and imports. This section has two Observable JavaScript cells, a slider and a list.`
// %%
viewof shop_price = Inputs.range([0, 10], { step: 0.5, value: 3, label: "price" })
// %%
shop_basket = [{ item: "tea", qty: 2 }, { item: "cake", qty: 1 }]
// %%
md`These SoundScript cells read them. A parameter that names a cell in another language must have a type. The notebook checks each value that arrives against that type. It then gives the cell a frozen copy. Thus the SoundScript cell holds a value of that type, and the other cell cannot change the value later. If a value does not agree with the type, the cell that reads it fails. The error names the place, for example \`rows[1].price: expected number, found string "12"\`. Cells that do not depend on the failed cell continue to run.

\`cheaper\` takes the slider as \`State<number>\`. Inside an action it can write to the slider. The slider moves, and every cell that reads \`shop_price\`, in either language, runs again. When the user moves the slider, that is one action call.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "type Line", to: "shopView" })
// %%
//ss: ./tutorial-ordinary.tsx
// %%
md`A parameter that reads an Observable JavaScript cell or a raw cell and has no type is \`[boundary-unannotated]\`. A type that the check cannot apply to a value, such as a \`Promise\` or a function inside an object, is \`[boundary-type]\`. A function type as the full annotation, such as \`measure: (s: string) => { n: number }\`, is accepted. The cell gets a function that checks each result and copies each argument. An action cannot capture that function. \`State\` on a cell with no \`viewof\` is \`[boundary-state]\`, unless the cell is a SoundScript state cell imported from another module.`
// %%
test_shop_total = {
  const el = await tutorial_module.value("viewof shop_price");
  const total = await tutorial_module.value("shopTotal");
  if (total !== 3 * el.value) throw new Error(`shopTotal is ${total} at price ${el.value}`);
  return `shopTotal ${total} at price ${el.value}`;
}
// %%
md`## How it works

This part tells you what the notebook does with a SoundScript cell. Each cell of this part describes one mechanism. These mechanisms are not part of the checked language. They are the compiler, the standard library and the editor hook in \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript\`, which has its own tab.`
// %%
md`### What a cell compiles to

The compiler turns each cell into one *variable* of the Observable runtime of the notebook. A variable is the object that holds a value and computes it again when an input changes. The table in section 6 lists every variable of its module. The variable of a plain cell has the name of the cell, and its inputs are the parameter names. A cell that calls a built-in such as \`action\` also takes the built-in as an input. The input is \`ss_action\`, an import from the runtime module.

The runtime decides when a cell runs. A cell runs when it is first read. It runs again when one of its inputs was computed again. The runtime does not compare values. Thus a cell also runs when an input was computed again to an equal value.

A cell that the checker rejects, such as the mistakes in section 8, does not run. Its variable raises the message from the checker.`
// %%
md`### A state cell is two variables

\`count\` in section 4 is two runtime variables, as an Observable \`viewof\` is. \`viewof count\` holds the handle. \`count\` holds the current value, and yields again after every committed write. A parameter \`count: State<number>\` is an input on \`viewof count\`, which does not change when the value changes. Thus in section 6 \`doubled\` runs again on **bump** and \`bump\` does not. The writes of one action call commit together after the call finishes. A cell that takes the \`State\` of a state cell in error is blocked.`
// %%
ss_highlightCode(md`### Capabilities are cells of the core

A capability parameter becomes an input on a variable of \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript\`. \`now\` is \`ss_now\`, \`random\` is \`ss_random\`, \`fetch\` is \`ss_fetch\`, and \`log\` is \`ss_log\`. \`invalidation\` is the \`invalidation\` of the runtime. \`pool\` is \`ss_pool\` of the add-on \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-workers\`. A module imports the add-on only if one of its cells takes \`pool\`. \`stamped\` in section 6 takes \`now\`, so its inputs include \`ss_now\`. \`compile\` is \`ss_compile\`. A module that compiles defines \`ss_compile\` itself, because \`compile\` reads the cells of that module:

\`\`\`
ss_compile = soundscript.compiler(() => ss_source(my_module))
viewof my_module = thisModule()
\`\`\``)
// %%
ss_highlightCode(md`### \`compile\` and its island

\`compile\` takes three lists of cell names:

\`\`\`
const inPool = (compile: Compile, pool: Pool) =>
  compile({
    inputs: ["y0", "y1"],                  // the function's argument: { y0, y1 }
    refs: ["width", "height", "maxIter"],  // read from the graph each time this cell runs
    outputs: ["rows"],                     // the result: Promise<{ rows }>
    options: { runtime: pool },            // a worker pool; leave it out to run on the main thread
  });
\`\`\`

The *island* is every cell upstream of the outputs. It stops at the inputs and refs, which are not part of it. Here the island is \`rows\`. Island cells are ordinary cells. On the main thread \`rows\` computes one row, for the defaults \`y0 = 0\` and \`y1 = 1\`.

**The type.** The checker computes the type from the cell graph. \`inPool\` is \`(args: { y0: number; y1: number }) => Promise<{ rows: Uint8ClampedArray }>\` (see its type column in section 7). \`image\` uses it without an annotation. Let \`C\` be the map of the module from cell name to value type. Then the type is \`(args: Pick<C, Inputs>) => Promise<Pick<C, Outputs>>\`. TypeScript cannot check that without \`C\`. Thus the checker gives the call its type, and the reference checker built on TypeScript cannot compare it. The names must be literal strings. Any other form is \`unsupported\`.

**The reactive link.** Each name in \`refs\` is a hidden input of the cell that calls \`compile\`, after its parameters. \`areaFn\` in section 6 takes \`scale\` this way. When you move the slider in section 7, \`maxIter\` changes. \`inPool\` runs again and returns a new function that contains the new value. \`image\` computes again because its input changed. The pool drops work that is still in the queue for the old function.

**The rules.** The fixtures of the checker test each of these rejections. A name must be a cell. An island cell must be accepted and synchronous, and must take no capability and no state cell (put the state cell in \`refs\`). Inputs, refs and outputs must survive \`structuredClone\` (no functions, state handles or actions). \`options.runtime\` must be a \`Pool\`. A call must agree with the computed argument type. \`compile\` does not run unless the checker accepts the calling cell and every island cell (\`test_compile_refuses_unchecked\`). There is no other guard. The code of the island runs in the worker with no sandbox, and only the checker keeps it to its inputs.

Measured in headless Chromium over ten page loads on 2026-11-14: the 640×420 image of section 7 takes about 64 ms on the main thread and about 10 ms on a pool of 8 workers. Both give the same bytes (\`test_compile_pool_matches_main\`).`)
// %%
md`### Listeners

A listener cell returns \`on(events, action)\`. While that value is the value of the cell, the notebook runs the action one time for each value that the events cell yields, in order. The notebook asks for the next value only after the action has run. Thus no value is skipped and no value is delivered two times. The listener ends when the cell runs again with other events or another action, when the cell is deleted, or when its module is disposed. Ordinary dataflow starts and stops the events: \`ticks\` reads \`playing\`, and the runtime restarts a generator cell when an input changes.`
// %%
md`### The renderer

Each JSX node becomes an \`h(tag, props, ...children)\` call. \`h\` returns a frozen \`{tag, props, children}\` value. To show a cell whose type is \`Elem\`, the page gives that value to the renderer (\`mount\` of the runtime module, called from \`ss_language.inspect\` of the core). Two checked cells of the runtime module, \`propOps\` and \`childOps\`, decide what a patch changes: which nodes are kept, moved, made or removed, and which props are written. The code that reads and writes the DOM is outside the checked language. The renderer makes the DOM one time. It then patches the DOM in place each time \`app\` gives a new value. The same tag at the same position keeps its node. An element that is the same object as last time is skipped. Children that all have \`key\` are matched by key. The renderer calls the actions when DOM events arrive. It changes each event into data (\`PointerData\` from a \`PointerEvent\`, a string from an \`input\`). That code is trusted code.

Section 9 uses this. \`bricks\`, \`ball\` and \`paddle\` each select one part of the world. \`bricksView\`, \`paddleView\` and \`ballView\` each draw one part. Each of these cells runs on every frame, because the runtime runs a cell when its input was computed again. \`mount\` keeps the cost of the page low: it changes only the DOM nodes whose description changed. A ball that moves is one \`style\` write. A broken brick removes one node. \`test_breakout_memo\` plays a copy of the game for 90 ticks and puts a limit on the DOM writes.

The board is made of \`div\`s that \`style\` strings position, because the checked JSX tags have no SVG and no canvas drawing. A transparent \`canvas\` on top supplies \`onPointerMove\`.`
// %%
md`### How the typed source is kept

The definition of a compiled variable is ordinary JavaScript. Its first comment is the marker \`/*{lang:"ss"}*/\`. The types are kept as \`/*ss:…*/\` comments. JSX is kept as \`/*ss<…*/\` comments next to its \`h(...)\` calls. The text around the function (the comments before it, \`const name = \`) is kept in a \`/*ss^{…}*/\` header. Thus the definition alone gives back the typed source. A definition survives anything that keeps the text of a function, such as a save of the notebook. \`test_ss_roundtrip\` checks every live variable: it decompiles, compiles again, and compares the text.`
// %%
md`### Editing
Shift-Enter always stores the edit. The checker checks the module again. Each cell is then ok, rejected (it breaks a rule) or blocked (it depends on a cell that is not ok). Only ok cells run. A rejected or blocked cell keeps its source and shows why it does not run. The notebook saves it in that condition. A state cell that stays ok keeps its value while you break and repair other cells. A new cell takes the language of the cell before it. The picker in the header of the editor shows the language and changes it. The language registers on the \`cell_languages\` plugin point of the copy of \`@tomlarkworthy/cell-map\` in this notebook. The editor and the visualizer read that plugin point.`
// %%
md`### Why four sections are modules

A module is one namespace. The example sources are shared with the test fixtures of the checker, so they keep their names. \`app\` is in five of them. There were two ways to make every section live. The first is to rename the cells of each section (\`reducer_step\`, \`game_step\`). That changes the text that the reader edits and that the fixtures check. The second is to give each section with a name conflict its own module. This notebook uses the second. The cost is that you edit sections 5, 6, 7 and 9 in their own modules, which the link under each table opens. This page imports the \`app\` of each section under another name (\`reducer_app\`, \`breakout_app\`).

The mistakes of section 2 stay in a box because \`width\` and \`area\` are names of section 1. \`bad_free_component\` stays in a box because a live cell would name a variable that does not exist.`
// %%
md`## What is guaranteed

For a cell that the checker accepts:

- The cell reads only its parameters and the allowed built-ins.
- While the cell runs, it writes only values that it made.
- A state cell changes only when the page calls an action.
- Every effect is a named parameter.
- A cell with no capability parameter returns the same value for the same inputs.

A cell that the checker rejects does not run. It shows the message from the checker. Every cell that depends on it shows the message too, until you repair the cell. The ordered list of action calls and their arguments decides the stored state. The stored state does not depend on which calls share a commit or on when cells run again.

Not guaranteed:

- The checker is a prototype. On 2026-11-16 the specification had 159 rejected sources. A reference checker built on the TypeScript compiler could judge 149 of them, and gave the stated rule for 116. The specification now has more rejected sources (434 on 2026-11-23). The checker here gives the stated rule for each of them ("How good is the checker").
- A cell can take \`now\` or \`random\` and be accepted. The cell is then not repeatable, and its signature shows that.
- An events cell that reads state restarts when it sees the state change. It sees two changes inside one commit as one change. Thus a clock that is paused and resumed in the same commit does not restart.`
// %%
md`## Language reference

There is one entry for each construct, as the checker checks it on 2026-11-24. The rules come from the v0 rule list of the language. Entries marked *lite* are additions in this checker and are not in that list. The name in brackets is the rule that a violation reports, as in the cell tables.`
// %%
md`### Cells

A source file holds cells and type declarations, nothing else (\`[module-level]\`).

| form | meaning |
|---|---|
| \`const x = (a, b) => …\` | a cell \`x\` with inputs \`a\` and \`b\`; \`let\`, a \`function\` expression and \`function x(a, b) {…}\` are the same |
| \`const x = async (a) => …\` | value type \`T\` for a body returning \`Promise<T>\` |
| \`const x = function* (a) {…}\` | a generator cell; value type \`Y\` for \`Generator<Y>\`; generators cannot be arrows |
| \`const x = (): StateCell<T> => state(v)\` | a state cell: value type \`T\` (see State) |
| \`const f = () => (n: number) => n * 2\` | a cell whose value is a function: two arrows, the outer one lists the inputs |
| \`type T = …\`, \`interface I {…}\`, \`import type\` | allowed at top level, not cells |

- A top-level \`const\` or \`let\` that is not a function is \`[cell-not-function]\`.
- A second cell with the name of another cell is \`[duplicate-cell]\`. It does not run, and the first cell keeps the name.
- Two cells that read each other are \`[cycle]\`.
- A cell named \`test_*\` is a test. It passes if it does not throw.`
// %%
md`### Inputs

Each parameter of a cell names the thing that the cell reads. The name and the annotation decide what the parameter receives:

| parameter | receives | a dependency on the value? |
|---|---|---|
| \`a\` or \`a: T\`, naming a cell | the cell's current value; an annotation must accept it (\`[inject-type]\`) | yes |
| \`count: State<T>\`, naming a state cell | the handle, with \`set\` and \`update\` and no read (\`[inject-state]\` if not a state cell of \`T\`) | no |
| \`ticks: Events<T>\`, naming a sync generator cell (*lite*) | a handle for \`on(...)\` (\`[inject-events]\` if not a generator yielding \`T\`) | no |
| a capability name (next entry) | the host's object for that effect | no |
| \`x: T\`, naming a cell in another language (section 10) | a frozen copy of its value, checked against \`T\` when it arrives; for a function type, a function that checks and copies each result (\`[boundary-unannotated]\` with no \`T\`, \`[boundary-type]\` if \`T\` cannot be checked) | yes |
| \`x: State<T>\`, naming an Observable JavaScript \`viewof x\` | the view as a handle: \`set\` and \`update\` write the control. A state cell of another module is taken the same way, imported as \`viewof x\`. (\`[boundary-state]\` if there is no \`viewof x\`) | no |
| \`x: State<T>\`, naming a SoundScript state cell imported from another module | the state cell in the module it is imported from | no |

- When a cell takes a generator as \`Events\`, a parameter that reads the generator without \`Events\` is \`[inject-events]\`, because it would receive the handle.
- No cell can take a listener cell (\`[listener-placement]\`).
- A name that is another variable of the module, in any language, is read through the checked boundary (section 10).
- Any other name is \`[inject-unknown]\`.
- A destructuring pattern is \`[inject-pattern]\`.
- A body cannot use the name of a cell that is not one of its parameters (\`[cell-by-name]\`).

Types:

- A parameter needs a type from an annotation or from its context (\`[untyped-parameter]\`).
- A type name that is not declared is \`[unknown-type]\`.
- Most other type errors, such as a \`string\` where a \`number\` is necessary, are \`[type]\`.
- A call with the wrong number of arguments is \`[arity]\`. Since 2026-11-25, a function with more parameters than its function type declares is also \`[arity]\`. Optional parameters and parameters with a default value are in the count. A rest parameter is \`[arity]\` when the type has no rest parameter. \`map\` passes the index and the array, so \`[5].map((x: number, i?: number, s?: string) => …)\` gives \`s\` an array. Remove the parameters that the type does not declare, or declare them in the type. A parameter that the function requires is also \`[arity]\` when the type has an optional or a rest parameter at the same position: a call through the type leaves it \`undefined\`.
- A callback declared \`(x: T) => void\` must return nothing. Thus \`(x) => x + 1\` is \`[type]\` there. A block body that returns nothing, such as \`(x) => { const y = x + 1; }\`, is accepted.
- \`forEach\` declares the result of its callback as \`unknown\`. Thus \`xs.forEach((x) => out.push(x))\` is accepted. A result that is a function or a promise is \`[forgets]\`.`
// %%
md`### Capabilities

Capabilities are reserved parameter names. The host supplies each capability that a cell takes.

| name | type | effect |
|---|---|---|
| \`now\` | \`Clock = () => number\` | the time in ms |
| \`random\` | \`Random = () => number\` | a random number |
| \`fetch\` | \`Fetch\` | network: \`(url, init?) => Promise<Mut<FetchResponse>>\` |
| \`log\` | \`Log = (...args) => void\` | a line in the program's log |
| \`invalidation\` | \`Promise<void>\` | resolves when this run of the cell is replaced |
| \`compile\` | \`Compile\` (*lite*) | turns a group of cells into a function (section 7) |
| \`pool\` | \`Pool\` (*lite*), \`{ size: number }\` | the page's worker pool, for \`compile\`'s \`runtime\`; from the add-on module |

A cell that takes no capability is deterministic.

You can read the body of a response only one time. Thus a response stays in the function that fetched it. The value of a cell, a state cell and a nested function cannot hold a response (S3.19). Read the body in that function and return the data, as \`rows\` does in the next example. A response has \`ok\`, \`status\`, \`text()\`, \`json()\` and \`arrayBuffer()\`. \`headers\`, \`statusText\`, \`blob\` and \`clone\` are *unsupported*. \`res.json()\` gives \`unknown\`. To use the result as data of a known type, check it with \`cast<T>(v)\` (S9.8):

\`\`\`ts
type Row = { readonly name: string; readonly price: number };
const rows = async (fetch: Fetch) => {
  const res = await fetch("rows.json");
  const r = cast<readonly Row[]>(await res.json());
  if (!r.ok) throw new Error("rows.json: " + r.error);
  return r.value;
};
\`\`\`

\`r.value\` is a frozen copy that holds only the members that \`Row\` declares. When the data is not a \`Row[]\`, \`r.path\` and \`r.error\` give the place, for example \`value[2].price: expected number, found string "3"\`. \`T\` must be plain data: numbers, strings, booleans, \`null\`, \`undefined\`, literal types, \`unknown\`, arrays, tuples, records and unions of these. A function, a method, \`Mut\`, a class, an index signature or a type parameter cannot be checked at run time. Each is \`[cast-type]\`.`
// %%
md`### Names a body may use

A body can use these names:

- Its parameters and its own locals.
- The prelude: \`state\`, \`action\`, \`snapshot\`, \`equals\`, \`read\`, \`messageOf\`, \`memo\`, \`cast\`, and \`on\` in *lite*. \`mut\`, \`freeze\` and \`borrow\` are names of removed calls (\`[removed-call]\`).
- These intrinsics: \`Object Array Math JSON Number String Boolean BigInt Symbol Map Set Error TypeError RangeError RegExp Promise\`, the typed arrays, \`ArrayBuffer DataView TextEncoder TextDecoder Infinity NaN undefined isNaN isFinite parseInt parseFloat encodeURIComponent decodeURIComponent encodeURI decodeURI atob btoa structuredClone URL URLSearchParams\`.

Any other name is \`[free-name]\`.

The v0 rules allow all of these intrinsics. The checker has no declarations yet for \`Symbol\`, \`DataView\`, \`TextEncoder\` and \`TextDecoder\`. A use of one of them is *unsupported*, and \`new\` on one of them is \`[new-non-intrinsic]\`. \`new Promise\` is also *unsupported*.

Some members of allowed objects are banned (\`[banned-member]\`), for example \`Math.random\`. A member that a declared type does not have is \`[no-member]\`. A member of an intrinsic that the declarations of the checker do not include is *unsupported*.

Not available: \`Date\`, \`console\`, \`window\`, \`document\`, \`globalThis\`, \`fetch\` (use the capability), timers, \`eval\`, \`Function\`. There is no replacement for \`Date\`. \`now()\` is the number of milliseconds since the page loaded. It is not a calendar time. To get a date, take an Observable JavaScript cell as a typed parameter.

- Generic functions and generic type aliases are accepted. You can write the type arguments or let the checker infer them.
- An \`extends\` bound on a type parameter is *unsupported*.
- In a \`.tsx\` source, write a generic arrow as \`<T,>(x: T) => …\`.
- \`?.\` and \`??\` are supported. \`??=\`, \`&&=\` and \`||=\` are *unsupported*.
- A \`switch\` that returns in a case for every member of a discriminated union returns on every path.

A cell cannot take a name that its compiled module already uses (\`[reserved-name]\`, S2.10). These names are:

- a capability: \`now random fetch log invalidation compile pool\`;
- an intrinsic from the list in this entry;
- a prelude function, \`h\` or \`Generators\`;
- a name that starts with \`ss_\`;
- a name that contains a \`$\`.

\`viewof\`, \`mutable\` and \`$\` followed by digits also cannot name a parameter or a local. \`h\` cannot be a local or a parameter of a cell that has JSX. Types and cells have separate namespaces. A top-level \`type\` or \`interface\` cannot take the name of a type of the checker, such as \`Listener\`, \`Pool\` or \`Array\` (\`[builtin-type-name]\`, S1.10). A type alias cannot be defined by itself with no object, array or tuple between, as in \`type Key = Key\` (\`[type-cycle]\`).`
// %%
md`### Immutable values

Values are read-only. The checker rejects these constructs:

| construct | rule |
|---|---|
| \`a.b = …\`, \`a[i] = …\`, \`++\`/\`--\`/\`+=\` on a member | \`[member-write]\` |
| \`push pop shift unshift splice sort reverse fill copyWithin set add delete clear\` on an array, map, set or typed array | \`[mutating-method]\` |
| \`delete a.b\` (allowed on a \`Mut\` when \`b\` is optional, or an index-signature entry named by a literal key) | \`[no-delete]\` |
| \`Object.assign(existing, …)\`; \`Object.assign({}, …)\` is reported unsupported, use a spread | \`[object-assign]\` |
| \`Object.freeze(x)\` where \`x\` is not an object or array literal | \`[object-freeze]\` |
| \`===\`, \`!==\`, \`==\`, \`!=\` on two non-primitive values; use \`equals(a, b)\` | \`[strict-eq-object]\` |
| a regexp with the \`g\` or \`y\` flag kept, returned or passed to anything but a String method: it keeps \`lastIndex\` | \`[regexp-state]\` |
| reassigning a \`const\` | \`[const-assign]\` |
| \`var\`, \`class\`, \`this\`, \`arguments\` | \`[no-var]\`, \`[no-class]\`, \`[no-this]\`, \`[no-arguments]\` |
| \`new\` on anything but an allowed intrinsic | \`[new-non-intrinsic]\` |
| \`any\`, \`as\` (except \`as const\`), \`x!\`, \`@ts-ignore\`, \`enum\`, \`namespace\`, \`import()\` | \`[no-any]\` (inside a cell; not checked in a top-level \`type\`), \`[no-cast]\`, \`[no-non-null]\`, \`[no-ts-ignore]\`, \`[no-enum]\`, \`[no-namespace]\`, \`[no-dynamic-import]\` |

Allowed: a local \`let\` and an assignment to it, loops, \`switch\`, \`throw\`, \`try\`/\`catch\`.

- A case body that can reach its end with another case after it is \`[switch-fallthrough]\`.
- A declaration directly in a case is \`[switch-declaration]\`. Put it in a block.
- A nested function can assign a \`let\` only if that function is a local helper that is called directly and never passed on (\`[let-capture]\`).
- A read of an iterator uses it up. Thus a function cannot capture a value whose type can hold an iterator (\`[iterator-capture]\`). These types are \`Iterable\`, the results of \`values()\`, \`keys()\` and \`entries()\`, \`Generator\`, and a type parameter. The value of a cell cannot hold an iterator (\`[iterator-cell]\`). Copy the iterator into an array with \`[...it]\`.
- A read of a property or an index of a value that can be \`undefined\` is \`[possibly-undefined]\`.

On this page an index read \`a[i]\` has the type \`T | undefined\`. Thus \`xs[0] * 2\` is rejected. The message names these corrections:

- write \`xs[0] ?? 0\`;
- check the value first: \`const x = xs[0]; if (x === undefined) return …\`;
- use \`for (const x of xs)\` in place of an index loop;
- read the last element with \`xs.at(-1)\`, which is also \`T | undefined\`, and check it.

(The comparison in "How good is the checker" uses \`T\`, as TypeScript does.)

A \`let\` or a parameter that a nested function assigns is not narrowed. After \`if (x !== null)\` it is still \`number | null\`, because a call can change it. Copy it to a \`const\` and check the \`const\`: \`const y = x; if (y !== null) …\`.

A record with an index signature, \`{ [k: string]: T }\` or \`Record<string, T>\`, takes only literal keys: \`m["x"]\` or \`m.x\`. A key that is known only when the cell runs, \`m[k]\`, is \`[banned-member]\`. Such a key can name an inherited member, for example \`toString\`, which is not an entry. Keep such keys in a \`Map<string, T>\` (\`m.get(k)\`, \`m.set(k, v)\`, \`m.has(k)\`).

The run time checks a value at each hand-over: the value of a cell, the arguments of an action, a value written to a state cell, and a value that a generator yields. At a hand-over:

- a response from \`fetch\` anywhere in the value throws;
- an async iterator anywhere in the value throws;
- a promise anywhere in the value of a cell throws, when the type of the cell names no promise.

The cell is then in error. The message names the value and the place: \`a response cannot leave its cell: … (the value of g, at value[0].r)\`. The type that the value had on its way, such as \`unknown\` or \`object\`, makes no difference. The checker reports the direct cases, and the run time stops the others. A call of an action returns \`undefined\`.

The checker has no model of these constructs and reports them as *unsupported*: \`for…in\`, \`instanceof\`, \`??=\` \`&&=\` \`||=\`, destructuring assignment (\`[a, b] = [b, a]\`), tagged templates, the comma operator, \`satisfies\`, a \`type\` declared inside a body, \`yield*\`, \`for await\`. In a function that holds a \`Mut\`, \`try/finally\` and labelled \`break\` are also *unsupported*.`
// %%
md`### Local mutation

A fresh value is an array or object literal, \`new\` on an intrinsic, or a copy. A local bound to a fresh value is a \`Mut<T>\`. The function that made it can write it.

- You can also write a fresh value in the expression that makes it. \`[...xs].sort()\` is accepted, and \`xs.sort()\` is not.
- \`xs.toSorted()\`, \`xs.toReversed()\`, \`xs.toSpliced(i, n)\` and \`xs.with(i, v)\` return a new array. No copy is necessary.
- An array, tuple or record annotation, or \`Map\`, \`Set\` or a typed array, keeps the local writable (\`const xs: number[] = []\`). Any other annotation, such as \`readonly number[]\`, a union or a type alias, makes the local read-only.
- \`Mut<T>\` is assignable to \`T\`. \`T\` is not assignable to \`Mut<T>\`.
- A helper can take a \`Mut<T>\` parameter and write it.
- The value that a cell returns or yields is frozen at run time, unless the checker has proved that nothing can write it later.

| rule | |
|---|---|
| a value that is not a \`Mut\` is not written | \`[member-write]\`, \`[mutating-method]\` |
| a cell's value and a cell's inputs never hold a \`Mut\` | \`[mut-cell]\` |
| \`Mut<…>\` is written only as the whole type of a parameter, a local or a type alias, not in a field, element, type argument, union or result; nor is \`typeof\` there | \`[mut-nested]\` |
| a function that outlives the call that made it does not capture a \`Mut\`, a local or parameter whose type holds a \`Mut\` anywhere (\`[xs, 1] as const\`), or a \`let\`, also only to read it; bind a \`const\` of a type with no \`Mut\` first (\`const c: readonly number[] = xs\`) and capture that | \`[mut-capture]\`, \`[let-capture]\` |
| a function does not write a local whose type holds a \`Mut\` after a state write (\`s.set\`, \`s.update\`) or a call of an action with arguments that it makes before it ends; finish the writes first | \`[member-write]\`, \`[mutating-method]\` |
| a function made while a body runs is not given to a state cell or an action before that body ends; at run time this throws, also where the checker does not see it; give data, or make the function in a cell of its own and take that cell as an input | \`[fn-publish]\` |
| a generator cell does not yield a typed array or a \`URL\` it can still write; these cannot be frozen, so yield a copy | \`[mut-yield]\` |
| in a generator, a local whose type holds a \`Mut\` anywhere, or a \`let\` of a non-primitive type, declared before a \`yield\` is not used after it; make it in the loop body | \`[mut-yield]\` |
| the same across an \`await\` in a generator or any function made inside a cell; only an async cell's own body is exempt | \`[mut-await]\` |
| \`mut\`, \`freeze\` and \`borrow\` were removed; delete the call | \`[removed-call]\` |
| prelude functions are only called, not used as values or declared again as locals | \`[prelude-value]\`, \`[prelude-shadow]\` |
| \`memo(f)\` gives the stored result for arguments it has seen; \`f\` takes plain data (no function, \`Mut\`, state, promise, generator, element or response), returns no promise, generator, iterator, \`Mut\` or state, and uses from outside itself only what an action body may, no capability, state handle or action, no enclosing function's parameter, and no const bound to a \`Mut\` | \`[memo-pure]\` |

\`snapshot(v)\` gives a copy of \`v\`. \`messageOf(e)\` gives the message of an \`Error\`, and \`String(e)\` for any other value. A caught value is \`unknown\`, so \`e.message\` is \`[no-member]\`. A cell cannot read itself. Thus a memoised function that calls itself gets a local name inside a cell: \`const fib = () => { const f = memo((n: number): number => (n < 2 ? n : f(n - 1) + f(n - 2))); return f; };\`.`
// %%
md`### State: \`state\`, \`action\`

| construct | meaning |
|---|---|
| \`const count = (): StateCell<number> => state(0)\` | a state cell; \`state(...)\` only as the returned value of a sync, non-generator cell (\`[state-placement]\`); its content may not admit \`undefined\` (\`[state-undefined]\`, S6.18); its content may not contain a \`State\` or \`StateCell\`, in a record, an array or a union (\`[state-in-state]\`) |
| \`count\` or \`count: number\` as a parameter | the current value; the reader runs again after each committed write |
| \`count: State<number>\` as a parameter | the handle: \`set(v)\`, \`update(f)\`, no read |
| \`action((by: number) => count.update((c) => c + by))\` | an \`Action<[number]>\`; writes are legal only lexically inside \`action(...)\` (\`[state-write]\`) |
| \`action(async () => …)\`, a generator, or a function that returns a promise | rejected: an action is synchronous (\`[action-async]\`); an async cell waits and a listener gives the result to an action |
| \`read(count)\` inside an action, with \`count: State<number>\` | the value as of this point in the call order: the stored value plus the writes made earlier in the same commit; legal only inside an action body (\`[read-outside-action]\`) |
| \`(other: State<number>, count: number) => action(() => other.set(count + 1))\` | rejected: an action body may not use a value that depends on state, that is a state cell's or an events cell's value, \`now\`, \`random\` or \`fetch\`, or a local computed from one (\`[action-capture]\`); \`log\` is allowed. A value from the clock or the random source reaches an action as an argument, from an events cell or a view, so it is recorded with the call: Breakout's \`ticks\` takes \`now\` and yields \`dt\`, and \`onTick\` passes it to \`tick\`. A \`button\`'s \`onClick\` gives no value, so a click carries no time; a \`canvas\` pointer event gives \`t\`. |

- A cell body never calls an action (\`[action-call]\`). An action can call another action inside its own body.
- A cell can return an action, put it in a record, or pass it to an \`Action\` parameter, a JSX event prop or \`on(...)\`. An action where a different type is expected is \`[action-pass]\`.
- The host calls the action. The writes of one action call commit together after the call returns.
- A \`State\` or \`Events\` handle given the type \`unknown\`, \`object\` or a type parameter of a function of the module is \`[handle-pass]\`.
- A record type can omit members of a record that it gets. A member of an object literal that the type does not declare is \`[excess-property]\`.
- A function, an array or a \`Map\` given a record type such as \`{}\` is \`[forgets]\`. A promise, a generator or an iterator given \`unknown\` or \`object\` is also \`[forgets]\`.
- A state cell can hold any data, also a \`Map\`, a \`Set\` or a function. It cannot hold a value that can be \`undefined\` (\`[state-undefined]\`: use \`null\`). It cannot hold another \`State\` (\`[state-in-state]\`).`
// %%
md`### Events and listeners: \`Events\`, \`on\` (*lite*)

| construct | meaning |
|---|---|
| \`const ticks = function* (now: Clock, playing: boolean) {…}\` | a sync generator cell taken as events; writes nothing; it runs again when an input changes; an async generator cannot be taken as \`Events\` yet |
| \`ticks: Events<number>\` | a handle to the generator, not its values; the same handle across the generator's runs |
| \`const onTick = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => on(ticks, tick)\` | a listener: \`tick\` runs once for each value of \`ticks\`; the action must accept what \`ticks\` yields (\`[type]\`) |
| \`flag ? on(a, x) : on(b, y)\` | a listener on one of two events cells |

- \`on(...)\` is legal only as the full value of a sync cell. It cannot be in a list or a record, in a state cell, in the return of a function, in an argument, or in a union with another value such as \`null\`. No cell can take a listener cell (\`[listener-placement]\`).
- The notebook pulls the generator only while a listener is attached. It pulls a sync generator one time for each animation frame.
- If an action throws, the notebook writes the error to the console as \`listener <cell>: action <action> threw on a value of events <events>\`. It discards the writes of the action and still delivers the next value.
- If the generator throws, the notebook writes the error to the console. The generator yields nothing more until it runs again.
- When two listener cells take the same events cell, each value goes to them in the order of the cells in the module. Listeners in other modules come after them. The order among modules is not specified (see the Not specified list of the specification). An edit or a new run of a listener does not change its position.

See section 9.`
// %%
md`### Workers: \`compile\` (*lite*)

\`compile({ inputs: [...], refs: [...], outputs: [...], options: { runtime: pool } })\`. Each list is a literal array of cell names. The *island* is the cells that are necessary to compute \`outputs\` from \`inputs\`. The value is a function of type \`(args: Pick<C, inputs>) => Promise<Pick<C, outputs>>\`, where \`C\` maps cell names to value types. The function runs on the pool when you give \`runtime\`. If not, it runs on the page.

When the cell that calls \`compile\` runs again (for example because a ref changed), the new function supersedes the old function. Calls of the old function that a worker already has finish and resolve with their own result. Calls of the old function that are still in the queue reject with a \`SupersededError\`. The runtime discards the value of an earlier run of a cell. Thus a cell such as \`image\` that awaits the function never shows a picture for an old input.

| rule | |
|---|---|
| island cells accepted, synchronous, no capability, no state cell (name it in \`refs\`) | \`[compile-island]\` |
| inputs, refs and outputs survive \`structuredClone\`: no functions, state handles or actions | \`[compile-boundary]\` |
| \`options.runtime\` is a \`Pool\` | \`[compile-options]\` |

Each name in \`refs\` is a hidden input of the calling cell, so a change gives a new function. \`compile\` does not run the function unless the checker accepts the calling cell and every island cell. See section 7.`
// %%
md`### Views: JSX (*lite*)

In a \`.tsx\` source, \`<tag prop={…}>children</tag>\` is a value of type \`Elem\`.

- Spread attributes (\`{...props}\`) are *unsupported*.
- A fragment \`<>…</>\` groups children.
- A component is a local function with a capital first letter that returns \`Elem\`: \`const Card = (p: { title: string }) => <div>{p.title}</div>\`, used as \`<Card title="x" />\`. A component that returns a different type is \`[type]\`.
- Allowed tags: \`div span p label strong fieldset legend output button input canvas\`. A tag that is not in the list, such as \`a ul li img select textarea section\`, is \`[no-member]\`.
- Props on all tags: \`class style title id key\`. On \`button\`: \`onClick disabled\`. On \`input\`: \`type name value min max step checked disabled onInput onClick\`. On \`canvas\`: \`width height onPointerDown onPointerMove onPointerUp onPointerCancel\`. A prop that the tag does not declare is \`[excess-property]\`. This includes \`data-*\` and \`aria-*\`.
- Event props take an \`Action\`. \`onClick\` takes an \`Action<[]>\`. \`onInput\` takes an \`Action<[string]>\`. Pointer events take an \`Action<[PointerData]>\` with \`x y t id buttons\`.
- \`key\` on the element of a component is for the renderer. It is not one of the props of the component.
- Children are elements, strings, numbers, booleans, \`null\`/\`undefined\`, or arrays of elements.

A handler made for each row, \`items.map((it) => <button onClick={action(() => remove(it.id))}>x</button>)\`, is \`[action-capture]\`. \`it\` comes from the value of a state cell, and an action body cannot use such a value (S6.9). Give the data of the row to the action as its argument: \`<input type="checkbox" value={String(it.id)} onInput={remove} />\` with \`remove: Action<[string]>\` gives \`remove\` the id of the row. A \`button\` has no value, so this form needs an \`input\`. Over an array literal or a \`readonly\` array that does not depend on state, a handler made for each row can use the row: \`[1, 2, 3].map((n) => <button onClick={action(() => pick.set(n))}>{n}</button>)\` is accepted (since 2026-11-25).

The full declarations are in the first appendix. Section 8 shows each rejection.`
// %%
md`## How good is the checker

These numbers compare the checker with a reference checker. The reference checker applies the same rules with the TypeScript compiler. The first two rows are from 2026-11-16. The other rows are from 2026-11-14. The fixtures and the three ports are not in this notebook. A port is a lopecode notebook written again in SoundScript.

\`\`\`
remaining fixtures      13 of 13 same verdict and reason
spec rejected sources   159; the reference can judge 149 and gives the stated rule for 116
                        (10 name cells in other languages; for the other 33 the reference
                         lacks the rule or reports a different or an extra diagnostic)
three ports             144 + 25 + 38 cells, all ok   (index reads typed T, as TypeScript does)
                        6 + 3 + 3 rejected            (index reads typed T | undefined)
173 mutants of ports    0 accepted that the reference rejects
                        2 rejected that the reference accepts (a number made a string, then used as a number)
                        12 unsupported (renamed intrinsic members)
fixture and port cells  5 of 503 accepted that the reference rejects: three helpers capturing a Mut
                        (allowed deliberately), and one cell each for an escaping Mut and a use after freeze
known slips             12 of 13 rejected
check time, CLI         53-63 ms lite, 282-331 ms the reference (whole process, median of 5)
\`\`\`

Measured in this page:`
// %%
sizes = htl.html`<table class="lite-panel">
<tr><td>parser attachment loaded by this page (acorn with its TypeScript plugin); the checker, compiler, renderer and standard library are cells</td><td>${(ss_bytes.raw / 1024).toFixed(0)} KiB, ${(ss_bytes.gz / 1024).toFixed(0)} KiB gzipped</td></tr>
<tr><td>section 7's 13 cells, check time in this browser, median of 20</td><td>${checkTime.toFixed(1)} ms</td></tr>
</table>`
// %%
md`These limits apply to all of this page:

- The checker has no \`keyof\`, indexed access types, tuple rest or variadic inference. A cell that needs one of these is \`unsupported\`. It is never \`ok\`.
- A module here holds the cells of one source file. The ports also have cells that are not checked and trusted foreign signatures. The checker reads these when it gets them. This page does not give them to the checker.
- Two parts of the renderer are trusted code: the code that changes an event into data (\`PointerData\` from a \`PointerEvent\`, a string from an \`input\`), and the code that reads and writes the DOM.
- A SoundScript cell can take a function from a cell in another language. The notebook checks each result of that function against its type. It does not check that the function gives equal results for equal arguments, that it ends, or that it writes nothing. The function is trusted for these.
- The reference checker does not read JSX. Thus only this checker checks a \`.tsx\` source, the JSX rules and the \`compile\` rules.`
// %%
md`## Not built

These items were discussed on 2026-11-14. None of them is in the checker or in this page.

- **Effects as tagged libraries.** \`state\`, \`action\`, \`Events\` and \`on\` are special cases inside the checker. The proposal moves them into declaration files. Tags in those files (\`@write\`, \`@handle\`, \`@cell\`) tell the checker what each function can do. A new type of effect would then need a library and no change to the checker.
- **Actors.** A library on those tags: an inbox cell, a post action, and the messages of the inbox as \`Events<M>\`. A \`mailbox\` primitive in the checker was proposed and rejected.
- **Label, do not reject.** The checker would accept \`Math.random\` and label the cell *unsound*, and would not reject it. It would also label every cell that takes a capability from that cell. A \`sound(...)\` marker would change the label back into an error.
- **Typed reflection.** A typed \`Variable\` and \`Module<C>\`. The checker would then check a cell that defines or redefines other cells, and a new definition would cause a check of the cells downstream. This needs \`keyof\`, bounds and indexed access, which the checker does not have.
- **Results kept across a reload.** The saved file would store the result of a deterministic cell. The notebook would use the stored result when the inputs are the same.
- **Export to ObservableHQ.** The compiled cells are ordinary JavaScript. The marker comments and the editor hook are only in this notebook.
- **\`now\` and \`random\` per cell.** Every cell that takes \`now\` gets the same \`ss_now\` of the core. Every cell that takes \`random\` gets the same \`ss_random\`. A recorded or seeded source for each cell is not built.`
// %%
md`## Tests

The tests do not run until **run the tests** is on. Each test of this module takes \`tests_on\`, which stays pending until then. There are two exceptions, \`test_half\` and \`test_shop_total\`. They are part of the examples of sections 1 and 10, and they run when the page loads. The Breakout tests play a copy of the cells of section 9 (\`ss_instance\`), not the game on this page. \`test_ss_counter\` presses **add one** and **clear** on a copy of the counter of section 4. Thus the counter on the page keeps its count. The tests of the section modules (\`test_undo\`, \`test_replay\`) are fast and always run. The tests of the core are in the core tab, with their own switch.`
// %%
viewof run_tests = {
  const toggle = Inputs.toggle({ label: "run the tests" });
  toggle.classList.add("lite-tests-toggle");
  return toggle;
}
// %%
tests_on = run_tests ? true : invalidation
// %%
testsView = testTable([["tutorial", tutorial_module], ["reducer", reducer_module], ["model", model_module], ["workers", workers_module], ["breakout", breakout_module]], invalidation)
// %%
test_examples_check_as_expected = {
  tests_on;
  const want = { cells: [7, 0], errors: [8, 4], mut: [4, 0], counter: [6, 0], reducer: [10, 0], model: [10, 0], workers: [13, 0], rejectJsx: [17, 11], game: [21, 0] };
  const got = {};
  for (const [k, [n, rejected]] of Object.entries(want)) {
    const r = soundscript.checkSources({ cells: examples[k], tsx: k !== "cells" && k !== "errors" && k !== "mut" });
    const bad = r.cells.filter((c) => c.status === "rejected").length;
    got[k] = [r.cells.length, bad];
    if (r.cells.length !== n || bad !== rejected) throw new Error(`${k}: ${r.cells.length} cells, ${bad} rejected; want ${n}, ${rejected}`);
  }
  return got;
}
// %%
test_compile_pool_matches_main = {
  tests_on;
  const image = await workers_image;
  const main = await workers_onMain({ y0: 0, y1: workers_height });
  if (main.rows.length !== image.length || !main.rows.every((v, i) => v === image[i])) throw new Error("pool and main thread differ");
  return `${image.length} bytes from ${workers_bands.length} pool calls equal one main-thread call`;
}
// %%
test_compile_refuses_unchecked = {
  tests_on;
  // the workers example with one island cell made to read Math.random, which the checker rejects
  const source = examples.workers.replace("out[at + 3] = 255;", "out[at + 3] = Math.random();");
  if (source === examples.workers) throw new Error("rows no longer writes out[at + 3] = 255; update this test");
  const c = soundscript.compiler(() => source);
  try {
    c.for("inPool", { width: 8, height: 8, maxIter: 10 })({ inputs: ["y0", "y1"], refs: ["width", "height", "maxIter"], outputs: ["rows"] });
  } catch (e) {
    if (!/^compile refused: (\w+, )*rows not ok/.test(e.message)) throw e;
    return e.message;
  }
  throw new Error("compile ran an island with a rejected cell");
}
// %%
runsOf = {
  // computations the runtime started for a variable since runsOf first saw it, the one it then held included:
  // the pending calls of an observer (runtime-sdk observe), one per computation and one on attaching
  const counts = new WeakMap();
  return (v) => {
    let c = counts.get(v);
    if (!c) { c = { n: 0 }; counts.set(v, c); observe(v, { pending: () => c.n++ }); }
    return c.n;
  };
}
// %%
test_breakout_listener = {
  tests_on;
  // its own copy of section 9's cells: the game on the page is not played or reset
  const game = await ss_instance(breakout_module, invalidation);
  // waits are counted in animation frames until a condition holds; the cap only turns a hung page into an error
  const frames = async (f, n = 600) => { for (let i = 0; i < n && !f(); i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r)); return f(); };
  const get = (n) => game._scope.get(n)._value;
  for (const n of ["reset", "toggle", "world", "bricks", "onTick"]) await game.value(n);
  // the clock of this copy only: other tests on the page pull generators of their own
  const w0 = get("world"), h0 = get("ticks");
  get("toggle")();
  let changes = 0, last = w0;
  await frames(() => { const w = get("world"); if (w !== last) { changes++; last = w; } return changes >= 10; });
  const playing = soundscript.pulling([h0]), w1 = get("world");
  get("toggle")();
  await frames(() => soundscript.pulling([h0]) === 0, 60);
  const w2 = get("world"), paused = soundscript.pulling([h0]);
  await frames(() => false, 10);
  const w3 = get("world");
  if (playing !== 1 || w1.ball.y === w0.ball.y) throw new Error(`playing: ${playing} clocks pulled, ball ${w0.ball.y} -> ${w1.ball.y}; a hidden tab gets no animation frames`);
  if (paused !== 0 || w3 !== w2) throw new Error(`paused: ${paused} clocks pulled, the world ${w3 === w2 ? "kept" : "changed"}`);
  // onTick runs again with each run of ticks, and stays one listener on the one handle
  const listeners = soundscript.running([h0]);
  if (listeners !== 1 || get("ticks") !== h0) throw new Error(`${listeners} listeners on ticks, same handle ${get("ticks") === h0}`);
  return `play: the clock runs and the ball moved ${w0.ball.y} -> ${w1.ball.y.toFixed(1)}; pause: it stops and the world stays the same object; ticks kept its handle, with one listener`;
}
// %%
test_breakout_memo = {
  test_breakout_listener; // both count clocks pulled, so they run one after the other
  const game = await ss_instance(breakout_module, invalidation);
  const get = (n) => game._scope.get(n)._value;
  for (const n of ["reset", "toggle", "world", "bricks", "app"]) await game.value(n);
  const runs = (n) => runsOf(game._scope.get(n));
  // the copy's app drawn into a node of its own, as breakout_view draws the page's
  let view = null;
  const drawn = new Promise((res) => invalidation.then(observe(game._scope.get("app"), { fulfilled(a) { view = soundscript.mount(a, view); res(); } })));
  await drawn;
  const muts = {};
  const mo = new MutationObserver((rs) => { for (const r of rs) { const k = r.type === "attributes" ? "attr " + r.attributeName : r.type; muts[k] = (muts[k] ?? 0) + (r.type === "childList" ? r.addedNodes.length + r.removedNodes.length : 1); } });
  const names = ["ball", "bricks", "paddle", "ballView", "bricksView", "paddleView", "status", "app", "ticks", "onTick"];
  mo.observe(view, { subtree: true, childList: true, attributes: true, characterData: true });
  names.forEach(runs);
  await null;
  const n0 = Object.fromEntries(names.map((n) => [n, runs(n)]));
  const b0 = get("bricks").length;
  // played for a number of delivered ticks and until a brick breaks, not for a time, so the counts do
  // not depend on the frame rate (step clamps a tick to at most 50 ms of game time)
  const TICKS = 90;
  let frames = 0, changes = 0, last = get("world");
  get("toggle")();
  // the frame cap only turns a hung page into an error
  const CAP = 3000;
  await new Promise((res) => { const f = () => { frames++; const w = get("world"); if (w !== last) { changes++; last = w; } if ((changes < TICKS || get("bricks").length === b0) && frames < CAP) requestAnimationFrame(f); else res(); }; requestAnimationFrame(f); });
  const h0 = get("ticks");
  get("toggle")();
  // the clock has stopped, and the mutations of the last tick have been delivered
  for (let i = 0; i < 60 && soundscript.pulling([h0]) > 0; i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
  await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
  mo.disconnect();
  const ran = Object.fromEntries(names.map((n) => [n, runs(n) - n0[n]]));
  const broken = b0 - get("bricks").length;
  if (changes < TICKS) throw new Error(`only ${changes} world changes in ${frames} frames; a hidden tab gets no animation frames`);
  if (broken < 1) throw new Error("no brick broke, so the keyed path was not exercised");
  // each view runs once for each run of its input (the runtime runs a cell when its input's variable runs,
  // equal value or not); the DOM takes only what changed: a style write per ball or paddle move, a node per
  // broken brick
  for (const [view, input] of [["ballView", "ball"], ["bricksView", "bricks"], ["paddleView", "paddle"]]) if (ran[view] > ran[input]) throw new Error(`${view} ran ${ran[view]} times for ${ran[input]} runs of ${input}`);
  if ((muts["attr style"] ?? 0) > ran.ballView + ran.paddleView + 2) throw new Error(`${muts["attr style"]} style writes for ${ran.ballView + ran.paddleView} ball and paddle draws`);
  if ((muts.childList ?? 0) > broken) throw new Error(`${muts.childList} nodes added or removed for ${broken} broken bricks`);
  return { frames, worldChanges: changes, broken, runs: ran, domMutations: muts };
}
// %%
md`## Appendix: the declarations JSX is checked against

The checker's own declarations, as this page's bundle carries them. A tag not listed is an error (\`<blink>\` in section 8).`
// %%
htl.html`<pre class="lite-src">${soundscript.JSX_DECLS.trim()}</pre>`
// %%
md`## Appendix: this notebook's code

Compiled cells take what they use from \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-runtime\`, one input per cell used (\`ss_action\`, \`ss_ref\`): the library that live cells call (state, actions, events, the JSX renderer), as ordinary cells. The checker and the per-cell compiler are the object \`soundscript\` of \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-checker\`, also ordinary cells, which this module's listings and tests use. The build generates both modules from the TypeScript sources. Only the parser, a third-party library, is a gzipped attachment. The example sources are attachments of this module, and the boxes and \`test_examples_check_as_expected\` read them; the live cells were compiled from the same files when the notebook was built.

Before each group of lesson cells is a \`sourceListing\` cell of this module. It shows the group's source, decompiled from the cells' live definitions and read again when the module's code changes, coloured with the editor's TypeScript parser (the core's \`ss_highlight\`). The lesson cells after it are shown as any cell is: a value with the normal Inspector, an action, a function, a listener, an events cell or a type as one line drawn by the core's \`ss_language.inspect\`, an element mounted, and a cell that does not check as its error.`
// %%
//raw: ss_bytes
// %%
checkTime = {
  const ts = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
    const t0 = performance.now();
    soundscript.checkSources({ cells: examples.workers, tsx: true });
    ts.push(performance.now() - t0);
  }
  return ts.sort((a, b) => a - b)[10];
}
// %%
examples = ({
  cells: await FileAttachment("01-cells.ts").text(),
  errors: await FileAttachment("02-errors.ts").text(),
  mut: await FileAttachment("03-mut.ts").text(),
  counter: await FileAttachment("04-counter.tsx").text(),
  reducer: await FileAttachment("05-reducer.tsx").text(),
  model: await FileAttachment("06-model.tsx").text(),
  workers: await FileAttachment("07-workers.tsx").text(),
  rejectJsx: await FileAttachment("08-reject-jsx.tsx").text(),
  game: await FileAttachment("09-breakout.tsx").text()
})
// %%
sourceListing = (module, invalidation, range) => {
  // the core's language-agnostic cellListing, in this page's listing style
  const pre = cellListing(module, invalidation, range);
  pre.classList.add("lite-code", "lite-listing");
  return pre;
}
// %%
badge = (status, text = status) => htl.html`<span class=${"lite-badge lite-badge-" + status}>${text}</span>`
// %%
isStale = (e) => typeof e === "function" && e.name === "variable_stale"
// %%
fmt = (v) => {
  if (typeof v === "function") return "ƒ";
  if (typeof v === "number") return Number.isNaN(v) ? "NaN" : String(v);
  if (v === undefined) return "undefined";
  if (ArrayBuffer.isView(v)) return `${v.constructor.name}(${v.length}) [${Array.from(v.slice(0, 8)).join(", ")}${v.length > 8 ? ", …" : ""}]`;
  if (v && typeof v === "object" && "tag" in v && "children" in v) return `<${v.tag || "fragment"}> element`;
  let s;
  try { s = JSON.stringify(v); } catch (e) { s = String(v); }
  return s === undefined ? String(v) : s.length > 90 ? s.slice(0, 87) + "..." : s;
}
// %%
sourceBox = (value, { rows } = {}) => {
  const box = Inputs.textarea({ value, rows: rows ?? value.split("\n").length + 1, monospace: true, spellcheck: false, width: "100%", resize: true });
  box.classList.add("lite-box");
  return box;
}
// %%
framed = (node) => {
  // the renderer patches only the props it set, so a class added here stays
  node.classList?.add("lite-app");
  return node;
}
// %%
depGraph = (module, invalidation, { only = null } = {}) => {
  // The module's cells as a graph drawn from the checker's facts: an arrow from each parameter's cell
  // to the cell that takes it. Redrawn when the module's code changes.
  const CAPS = new Set(["now", "random", "fetch", "log", "invalidation", "compile", "pool"]);
  const holder = htl.html`<figure class="lite-graph">`, uid = Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8);
  const svgEl = (tag, attrs = {}, text) => {
    const e = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", tag);
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(attrs)) e.setAttribute(k, v);
    if (text !== undefined) e.textContent = text;
    return e;
  };
  // layers run left to right, or top to bottom (wrapped to 340 px) when the container is narrower than
  // three quarters of the left-to-right drawing; a ResizeObserver redraws when that choice changes
  let vertical = null, wide = 0;
  const draw = () => {
    let result;
    try { result = ss_check(module).result; } catch (e) { holder.replaceChildren(htl.html`<div class="lite-err">${e.message}</div>`); return; }
    const cells = result.cells.filter((c) => !only || only.includes(c.name));
    const names = new Set(cells.map((c) => c.name));
    const kindOf = (c) => {
      const f = result.facts.get(c.name);
      if (c.status !== "ok") return "rejected";
      if (f?.returnsRef) return "ref";
      if (/^Action\b/.test(c.value ?? "")) return "action";
      if (/^Listener\b/.test(c.value ?? "")) return "listener";
      if (f?.generator) return "generator";
      if (c.name.startsWith("test_")) return "test";
      return "cell";
    };
    const nodes = new Map(cells.map((c) => [c.name, { name: c.name, kind: kindOf(c), ins: [], outs: [] }]));
    const edges = [];
    for (const c of cells) {
      const f = result.facts.get(c.name);
      for (const p of f?.params ?? []) {
        if (CAPS.has(p.name)) {
          if (!nodes.has(p.name)) nodes.set(p.name, { name: p.name, kind: "cap", ins: [], outs: [] });
        } else if (!names.has(p.name)) continue;
        edges.push({ from: p.name, to: c.name, kind: p.refTyped ? "handle" : p.eventsTyped ? "events" : "value" });
      }
      for (const spec of f?.compiles ?? []) for (const r of spec.refs) if (names.has(r)) edges.push({ from: r, to: c.name, kind: "refs" });
    }
    for (const e of edges) { nodes.get(e.from).outs.push(e.to); nodes.get(e.to).ins.push(e.from); }
    // layers by longest path from a cell with no inputs; then a few sweeps ordering each layer by its neighbours
    const layer = new Map();
    const depth = (n, seen = new Set()) => {
      if (layer.has(n)) return layer.get(n);
      if (seen.has(n)) return 0;
      seen.add(n);
      const d = Math.max(0, ...nodes.get(n).ins.map((i) => depth(i, seen) + 1));
      layer.set(n, d);
      return d;
    };
    for (const n of nodes.keys()) depth(n);
    const layers = [];
    for (const [n, d] of layer) (layers[d] ??= []).push(n);
    const pos = new Map();
    const index = () => layers.forEach((l) => l.forEach((n, i) => pos.set(n, i / Math.max(1, l.length - 1))));
    const mean = (xs, at) => (xs.length ? xs.reduce((a, x) => a + pos.get(x), 0) / xs.length : at);
    index();
    for (let sweep = 0; sweep < 4; sweep++) {
      for (const l of sweep % 2 ? [...layers].reverse() : layers) {
        const key = new Map(l.map((n) => [n, mean(sweep % 2 ? nodes.get(n).outs : nodes.get(n).ins, pos.get(n))]));
        l.sort((a, b) => key.get(a) - key.get(b));
        index();
      }
    }
    const COL = 150, ROW = 32, H = 22, PAD = 6, GAP = 10, MAXW = 340;
    const widthOf = (n) => Math.min(COL - 22, 14 + 7.4 * n.length);
    const rows = Math.max(...layers.map((l) => l.length));
    wide = layers.length * COL - 22 + 2 * PAD;
    vertical ??= (holder.clientWidth || innerWidth - 32) < wide * 0.75;
    const at = new Map();
    let W, Ht;
    if (!vertical) {
      W = wide;
      Ht = rows * ROW + 2 * PAD;
      layers.forEach((l, d) => l.forEach((n, i) => at.set(n, { x: PAD + d * COL, y: PAD + ((rows - l.length) / 2 + i) * ROW, w: widthOf(n) })));
    } else {
      const lines = [];
      for (const l of layers) {
        let line = [], used = 0;
        for (const n of l) {
          if (line.length && used + GAP + widthOf(n) > MAXW) { lines.push(line); line = []; used = 0; }
          used += (line.length ? GAP : 0) + widthOf(n);
          line.push(n);
        }
        if (line.length) lines.push(line);
      }
      const spanOf = (line) => line.reduce((a, n) => a + widthOf(n), 0) + GAP * (line.length - 1);
      W = Math.max(...lines.map(spanOf)) + 2 * PAD;
      lines.forEach((line, r) => {
        let x = PAD + (W - 2 * PAD - spanOf(line)) / 2;
        for (const n of line) { at.set(n, { x, y: PAD + r * (ROW + 12), w: widthOf(n) }); x += widthOf(n) + GAP; }
      });
      Ht = lines.length * (ROW + 12) - 12 + 2 * PAD;
    }
    const svg = svgEl("svg", { viewBox: `0 0 ${W} ${Ht}`, width: W, role: "img", "aria-label": `dependency graph of ${[...nodes.keys()].join(", ")}` });
    svg.style.maxWidth = "100%";
    svg.style.height = "auto";
    const defs = svgEl("defs");
    for (const k of ["value", "handle", "events", "refs"]) {
      const m = svgEl("marker", { id: `lite-arrow-${k}-${uid}`, viewBox: "0 0 8 8", refX: 7, refY: 4, markerWidth: 7, markerHeight: 7, orient: "auto-start-reverse", class: `lite-arrow-${k}` });
      m.append(svgEl("path", { d: "M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z" }));
      defs.append(m);
    }
    svg.append(defs);
    for (const e of edges) {
      const a = at.get(e.from), b = at.get(e.to);
      const d = vertical
        ? (() => { const x1 = a.x + a.w / 2, y1 = a.y + H, x2 = b.x + b.w / 2, y2 = b.y - 2, my = (y1 + y2) / 2; return `M${x1},${y1} C${x1},${my} ${x2},${my} ${x2},${y2}`; })()
        : (() => { const x1 = a.x + a.w, y1 = a.y + H / 2, x2 = b.x - 2, y2 = b.y + H / 2, mx = (x1 + x2) / 2; return `M${x1},${y1} C${mx},${y1} ${mx},${y2} ${x2},${y2}`; })();
      svg.append(svgEl("path", { d, class: `lite-edge lite-edge-${e.kind}`, "marker-end": `url(#lite-arrow-${e.kind}-${uid})` }));
    }
    for (const [n, node] of nodes) {
      const p = at.get(n);
      const g = svgEl("g", { class: `lite-node lite-node-${node.kind}` });
      g.append(svgEl("title", {}, `${n}: ${node.kind}`));
      g.append(svgEl("rect", { x: p.x, y: p.y, width: p.w, height: H, rx: node.kind === "action" || node.kind === "cap" ? 11 : 4 }));
      g.append(svgEl("text", { x: p.x + p.w / 2, y: p.y + H / 2 + 4, "text-anchor": "middle" }, n));
      svg.append(g);
    }
    const kinds = new Set([...nodes.values()].map((x) => x.kind)), kindsE = new Set(edges.map((e) => e.kind));
    const KEY = { cell: "cell", test: "test", ref: "state cell", action: "action", generator: "generator", listener: "listener", cap: "capability", rejected: "rejected" };
    const EKEY = { value: "reads the value", handle: "gets the handle (State)", events: "gets the handle (Events)", refs: "compile refs" };
    holder.replaceChildren(
      htl.html`<div class="lite-graph-scroll">${svg}</div>`,
      htl.html`<figcaption>Arrows run from a cell to the cells that take it as a parameter, from the checker's facts for this module. ${[...kinds].map((k) => htl.html`<span class=${"lite-key lite-node-" + k}><i></i>${KEY[k]}</span>`)} ${[...kindsE].filter((k) => k !== "value").map((k) => htl.html`<span class=${"lite-key lite-edge-" + k}><b></b>${EKEY[k]}</span>`)}</figcaption>`
    );
  };
  let queued = false;
  const off = onCodeChange(({ variable, previous }) => {
    if ((variable ?? previous)?._module !== module || queued) return;
    queued = true;
    queueMicrotask(() => { queued = false; draw(); });
  });
  const resized = new ResizeObserver(() => {
    if (!holder.clientWidth || !wide) return;
    const next = holder.clientWidth < wide * 0.75;
    if (next !== vertical) { vertical = next; draw(); }
  });
  resized.observe(holder);
  invalidation.then(() => { off(); resized.disconnect(); });
  draw();
  return holder;
}
// %%
testTable = (modules, invalidation) => {
  // every test_* variable of the given modules, with its state as it arrives; rebuilt when their code changes
  const body = htl.html`<tbody>`, summary = htl.html`<div class="lite-summary">`;
  let cancels = [], queued = false, generation = 0;
  const rebuild = () => {
    queued = false;
    cancels.forEach((c) => c());
    cancels = [];
    const mine = ++generation;
    const rows = [];
    const tally = () => {
      const n = (s) => rows.filter((r) => r.state === s).length;
      summary.replaceChildren(`${rows.length} tests: `, badge("pass", `${n("pass")} pass`), " ", badge("fail", `${n("fail")} fail`), " ", badge("pending", `${n("pending")} not run`));
    };
    for (const [label, m] of modules) for (const v of runtime._variables) {
      if (v._module !== m || typeof v._name !== "string" || !v._name.startsWith("test_")) continue;
      const state = htl.html`<td>`, value = htl.html`<td class="v">`;
      const row = { state: "pending" };
      const set = (s, text) => { if (mine !== generation) return; row.state = s; state.replaceChildren(badge(s, s === "pending" ? "not run" : s)); value.textContent = text; tally(); };
      set("pending", "");
      rows.push(row);
      body.append(htl.html`<tr><td>${label}</td><td><code>${v._name}</code></td>${state}${value}</tr>`);
      cancels.push(observe(v, { fulfilled: (x) => set("pass", fmt(x)), rejected: (e) => set("fail", String(e?.message ?? e)), pending: () => set("pending", "") }));
      // a promise superseded by a newer run rejects with the runtime's variable_stale; that is not a failure
      v._promise?.then((x) => v._value !== undefined && set("pass", fmt(x)), (e) => isStale(e) || set("fail", String(e?.message ?? e)));
    }
    tally();
  };
  const off = onCodeChange(({ variable, previous }) => {
    const m = (variable ?? previous)?._module;
    if (!modules.some(([, x]) => x === m) || queued) return;
    queued = true;
    queueMicrotask(() => { body.replaceChildren(); rebuild(); });
  });
  rebuild();
  invalidation.then(() => { off(); cancels.forEach((c) => c()); });
  return htl.html`<div class="lite-tests">${summary}<div class="lite-scroll"><table class="lite-panel"><thead><tr><th>module</th><th>test</th><th>state</th><th>value</th></tr></thead>${body}</table></div></div>`;
}
// %%
checkBox = (src, { tsx = false } = {}) => {
  let checked;
  try { checked = soundscript.checkSources({ cells: src, tsx }); }
  catch (e) { return htl.html`<div class="lite-panel lite-err">${e.message}</div>`; }
  const lines = src.split("\n");
  const body = htl.html`<tbody>`;
  for (const c of checked.cells) {
    body.append(htl.html`<tr class=${"lite-" + c.status}><td><code>${c.name}</code></td><td>${badge(c.status)}</td><td><code>${c.value ?? ""}</code></td></tr>`);
    const problems = [...c.errors, ...c.unsupported.map((u) => ({ ...u, rule: "unsupported" }))];
    if (c.status === "blocked") problems.push({ line: c.line, rule: "blocked", msg: `by ${c.blockedBy.join(", ")}` });
    for (const e of problems)
      body.append(htl.html`<tr class="lite-err"><td></td><td colspan="2">line ${e.line} <b>[${e.rule}]</b> ${e.msg}<br><code>${(lines[e.line - 1] ?? "").trim()}</code></td></tr>`);
  }
  const count = (s) => checked.cells.filter((c) => c.status === s).length;
  return htl.html`<div class="lite-lesson"><div class="lite-summary">${checked.cells.length} cells: ${count("ok")} ok, ${count("rejected")} rejected, ${count("unsupported")} unsupported, ${count("blocked")} blocked. Checked in ${checked.ms.toFixed(1)} ms.</div>${checked.module.map((e) => htl.html`<div class="lite-err">line ${e.line} <b>[${e.rule}]</b> ${e.msg}</div>`)}
<div class="lite-scroll"><table class="lite-panel"><thead><tr><th>cell</th><th>check</th><th>type</th></tr></thead>${body}</table></div></div>`;
}
// %%
kindOf = (v) => {
  const d = String(v._definition);
  if (v._name.startsWith("viewof ")) return "state cell: the handle";
  if (d === soundscript.STATE_VALUE) return "state cell: the value";
  // the compiled shape, from its parse
  const k = soundscript.compiledKind(d);
  if (k?.kind === "events") return "events: a handle, pulled only while a listener is attached";
  if (k?.callee === "listen") return "cell, a listener";
  if (k?.callee === "rejected") return "rejected: raises the checker's messages";
  if (k?.callee !== "plain" && k?.callee !== "apply") return "type";
  const extra = k.refs.length ? ", compile refs as inputs" : k.callee === "apply" ? ", taking runtime cells (ss_<name>)" : "";
  return `cell: its function applied to its inputs${extra}`;
}
// %%
liveRows = (module, invalidation, { head, cells, row, cls = "" }) => {
  // A table of module's SoundScript variables, drawn on change only: all rows when the module's code
  // changes (onCodeChange), one row's live part when its variable computes (observe).
  const body = htl.html`<tbody>`;
  let cancels = [], rebuildQueued = false;
  const rebuild = () => {
    rebuildQueued = false;
    cancels.forEach((c) => c());
    const made = cells().map((r) => ({ ...r, ...row(r) }));
    body.replaceChildren(...made.map((r) => r.tr));
    // the runtime keeps no error field, so a row learns of an error from the variable's promise
    cancels = made.map((r) => observe(r.variable, { fulfilled: () => r.update(), rejected: (e) => r.update(e ?? "error") }));
    for (const r of made) r.variable._promise?.then(() => {}, (e) => isStale(e) || r.update(e ?? "error"));
  };
  const queue = () => { if (!rebuildQueued) { rebuildQueued = true; queueMicrotask(rebuild); } };
  const off = onCodeChange(({ variable, previous }) => { if ((variable ?? previous)?._module === module) queue(); });
  rebuild();
  invalidation.then(() => { off(); cancels.forEach((c) => c()); });
  return htl.html`<div class="lite-scroll"><table class=${"lite-panel " + cls}>${head}${body}</table></div>`;
}
// %%
moduleTable = (module, invalidation) => {
  // verdicts come from the module's source, checked again when its code changes
  const verdicts = () => ss_check(module).result;
  return liveRows(module, invalidation, {
    head: htl.html`<thead><tr><th>cell</th><th>check</th><th>type</th><th>value</th><th>runs</th></tr></thead>`,
    cells: () => { const res = verdicts(); return ss_moduleCells(module).filter((c) => !c.key.startsWith("type ")).map((c) => ({ ...c, variable: module._scope.get(soundscript.variableName(c.key)) ?? c.variable, verdict: res.cells.find((x) => x.name === c.key) })); },
    row: ({ key, variable: v, verdict: r }) => {
      const val = htl.html`<td class="v">`, runs = htl.html`<td>`;
      const update = (error) => {
        val.textContent = error !== undefined ? `throws: ${error?.message ?? error}` : fmt(v._value);
        runs.textContent = String(runsOf(module._scope.get(soundscript.handleName(key)) ?? v));
      };
      update();
      return { update, tr: htl.html`<tr class=${"lite-" + (r?.status ?? "blocked")}><td><code>${key}</code></td><td>${r ? badge(r.status) : ""}</td><td><code>${r?.value ?? ""}</code></td>${val}${runs}</tr>` };
    },
  });
}
// %%
variableTable = (module, invalidation) => liveRows(module, invalidation, {
  cls: "lite-vars",
  head: htl.html`<thead><tr><th>variable</th><th>inputs</th><th>kind</th><th>runs</th></tr></thead>`,
  cells: () => [...runtime._variables].filter((v) => v._module === module && typeof v._name === "string" && ss_isSs(v)).map((variable) => ({ variable })),
  row: ({ variable: v }) => {
    const runs = htl.html`<td>`;
    const update = () => { runs.textContent = String(runsOf(v)); };
    update();
    return { update, tr: htl.html`<tr><td><code>${v._name}</code></td><td><code>${v._inputs.map((i) => i._name).join(", ")}</code></td><td>${kindOf(v)}</td>${runs}</tr>` };
  },
})
// %%
benchmark = {
  remeasure;
  const bands = workers_bands, inPool = workers_inPool, onMain = workers_onMain;
  await workers_image;
  const run = (f) => Promise.all(bands.map((b) => f(b)));
  const time = async (f) => { const t0 = performance.now(); await run(f); return performance.now() - t0; };
  const a = await run(inPool), b = await run(onMain);
  const same = a.every((r, i) => r.rows.length === b[i].rows.length && r.rows.every((v, j) => v === b[i].rows[j]));
  for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) { await run(inPool); await run(onMain); }
  const runs = 7, main = [], pool = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < runs; i++) { main.push(await time(onMain)); pool.push(await time(inPool)); }
  const median = (xs) => [...xs].sort((x, y) => x - y)[Math.floor(xs.length / 2)];
  return { poolSize: workers_poolSize, hardware: navigator.hardwareConcurrency, bands: bands.length, runs, mainMs: median(main), poolMs: median(pool), same };
}
// %%
style = htl.html`<style>
:root {
  --lite-ok: #2f9e44;
  --lite-bad: var(--theme-error, #d6336c);
  --lite-warn: #c98a12;
  --lite-accent: var(--theme-foreground-focus, #3b6fd8);
  /* text in these colours, mixed toward the foreground so it reads at 4.5:1 on dark and light themes */
  --lite-ok-text: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-ok) 55%, var(--theme-foreground));
  --lite-bad-text: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-bad) 55%, var(--theme-foreground));
  --lite-warn-text: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-warn) 55%, var(--theme-foreground));
  /* the theme's muted text, 30% toward the foreground: the light theme's own is 3.2 to 4.4:1 on the alt background */
  --lite-muted-text: color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-foreground-muted) 70%, var(--theme-foreground));
}
${ss_hl_css}
.lite-err b { color: var(--lite-bad-text); }
.lite-badge { display: inline-block; padding: 0 7px; border-radius: 9px; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 18px; white-space: nowrap; border: 1px solid currentColor; }
.lite-badge-ok, .lite-badge-pass { color: var(--lite-ok-text); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-ok) 14%, transparent); }
.lite-badge-rejected, .lite-badge-fail { color: var(--lite-bad-text); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-bad) 14%, transparent); }
.lite-badge-unsupported, .lite-badge-blocked { color: var(--lite-warn-text); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-warn) 14%, transparent); }
.lite-badge-pending { color: var(--lite-muted-text); }
.lite-code { margin: 0; padding: 8px 10px; font: 13px/1.45 var(--monospace, monospace); color: var(--theme-foreground); background: none; overflow-x: auto; white-space: pre; tab-size: 2; }
.lite-listing { max-height: 420px; overflow: auto; border: 1px solid var(--theme-foreground-faint); border-radius: 4px; background: var(--theme-background-alt); }
.lp2-pane .ss-view { padding: 8px 10px; margin: 6px 0; border: 1px solid var(--theme-foreground-faint); border-radius: 6px; background: var(--theme-background-alt); }
.lp2-pane .ss-view .ss-view { padding: 0; margin: 0; border: none; background: none; }
.ss-view button { font: 13px/1.2 var(--sans-serif, system-ui); padding: 5px 12px; color: var(--theme-foreground); background: var(--theme-background-alt); border: 1px solid var(--theme-foreground-faint); border-radius: 5px; cursor: pointer; }
.ss-view button:hover { border-color: var(--lite-accent); }
.ss-view output { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  /* a listing wraps on a phone, keeping its indentation, rather than scrolling out of sight */
  .lite-listing { white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
}
.lite-panel { border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 13px; width: 100%; max-width: 100%; }
.lite-panel th { text-align: left; color: var(--lite-muted-text); font-weight: 600; }
.lite-panel td, .lite-panel th { padding: 3px 8px 3px 0; vertical-align: top; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--theme-foreground-faintest); }
.lite-panel td code { white-space: nowrap; }
.lite-panel td.v { font-family: var(--monospace, monospace); overflow-wrap: anywhere; min-width: 12ch; }
.lite-panel tr.lite-rejected > td:first-child { box-shadow: inset 3px 0 0 var(--lite-bad); padding-left: 6px; }
.lite-panel tr.lite-unsupported > td:first-child, .lite-panel tr.lite-blocked > td:first-child { box-shadow: inset 3px 0 0 var(--lite-warn); padding-left: 6px; }
.lite-panel tr.lite-rejected { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-bad) 7%, transparent); }
.lite-panel tr.lite-err td { border-bottom: none; color: var(--theme-foreground); font-size: 12px; padding-top: 0; }
.lite-panel tr.lite-err + tr:not(.lite-err) td { border-top: 1px solid var(--theme-foreground-faintest); }
.lite-summary, .lite-readout { font-size: 13px; margin: 6px 0; color: var(--lite-muted-text); }
.lite-readout { font-family: var(--monospace, monospace); color: var(--theme-foreground); }
.lite-game canvas { touch-action: none; }
.observablehq[cell="workers_maxIterAt"], .observablehq[cell="workers_maxIterAt"] + div { display: none; }
.lite-mandel { display: block; max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 6px 0; border-radius: 4px; }
.lite-vars { margin-top: 10px; }
.lite-app { padding: 8px 10px; margin: 6px 0; border: 1px solid var(--theme-foreground-faint); border-radius: 6px; background: var(--theme-background-alt); }
.lite-app output { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.lite-app input[type=range] { vertical-align: middle; margin: 0 6px; max-width: min(240px, 50vw); }
.lite-scroll, .lite-graph-scroll { overflow-x: auto; max-width: 100%; }
.lite-src { font-size: 12px; max-height: 320px; overflow: auto; background: var(--theme-background-alt); padding: 6px; }
.lite-box textarea { font-size: 12px; }
.lite-graph { margin: 8px 0; max-width: 100%; }
.lite-graph svg { display: block; font: 12px var(--monospace, monospace); }
.lite-graph figcaption { font-size: 12px; color: var(--lite-muted-text); margin-top: 4px; line-height: 1.8; }
.lite-node rect { fill: var(--theme-background-alt); stroke: var(--theme-foreground-faint); stroke-width: 1.2; }
.lite-node text { fill: var(--theme-foreground); }
.lite-node-ref rect { fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-accent) 22%, var(--theme-background-alt)); stroke: var(--lite-accent); stroke-width: 2; }
.lite-node-action rect { fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-warn) 18%, var(--theme-background-alt)); stroke: var(--lite-warn); }
.lite-node-listener rect { fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-ok) 18%, var(--theme-background-alt)); stroke: var(--lite-ok); stroke-width: 2; }
.lite-node-generator rect { stroke: var(--lite-ok); stroke-dasharray: 4 2; }
.lite-node-cap rect { fill: none; stroke: var(--theme-foreground-muted); stroke-dasharray: 2 2; }
.lite-node-cap text { fill: var(--lite-muted-text); font-style: italic; }
.lite-node-test rect { stroke-dasharray: 1 3; }
.lite-node-rejected rect { stroke: var(--lite-bad); stroke-width: 2; }
.lite-edge { fill: none; stroke: var(--theme-foreground-faint); stroke-width: 1.2; }
.lite-edge-handle { stroke: var(--lite-accent); stroke-dasharray: 5 3; }
.lite-edge-events { stroke: var(--lite-ok); stroke-dasharray: 5 3; }
.lite-edge-refs { stroke-dasharray: 2 3; }
.lite-arrow-value path, .lite-arrow-refs path { fill: var(--theme-foreground-faint); }
.lite-arrow-handle path { fill: var(--lite-accent); }
.lite-arrow-events path { fill: var(--lite-ok); }
.lite-key { margin-right: 10px; white-space: nowrap; }
.lite-key i { display: inline-block; width: 14px; height: 10px; margin-right: 4px; vertical-align: -1px; border: 1.2px solid var(--theme-foreground-faint); border-radius: 2px; background: var(--theme-background-alt); }
.lite-key.lite-node-ref i { border: 2px solid var(--lite-accent); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-accent) 22%, var(--theme-background-alt)); }
.lite-key.lite-node-action i { border-color: var(--lite-warn); border-radius: 6px; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-warn) 18%, var(--theme-background-alt)); }
.lite-key.lite-node-listener i { border: 2px solid var(--lite-ok); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-ok) 18%, var(--theme-background-alt)); }
.lite-key.lite-node-generator i { border: 1.2px dashed var(--lite-ok); }
.lite-key.lite-node-cap i { border: 1.2px dashed var(--theme-foreground-muted); background: none; border-radius: 6px; }
.lite-key.lite-node-test i { border-style: dotted; }
.lite-key.lite-node-rejected i { border: 2px solid var(--lite-bad); }
.lite-key b { display: inline-block; width: 18px; margin-right: 4px; vertical-align: 3px; border-top: 2px dashed var(--theme-foreground-faint); }
.lite-key.lite-edge-handle b { border-color: var(--lite-accent); }
.lite-key.lite-edge-events b { border-color: var(--lite-ok); }
.lite-key.lite-edge-refs b { border-top-style: dotted; }
.lite-game { --k: 1; position: relative; display: inline-block; max-width: 100%; }
.lite-game > div:first-child > div:first-child { border-radius: 4px; box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--theme-foreground-faintest); transform: scale(var(--k)); transform-origin: 0 0; margin: 0 calc(320px * (var(--k) - 1)) calc(240px * (var(--k) - 1)) 0; }
.lite-game output { display: block; margin: 8px 0 6px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; }
.lite-game button, .lite-app button, .lite-bench-button button { font: 13px/1.2 var(--sans-serif, system-ui); padding: 5px 12px; color: var(--theme-foreground); background: var(--theme-background-alt); border: 1px solid var(--theme-foreground-faint); border-radius: 5px; cursor: pointer; }
.lite-game button:hover, .lite-app button:hover { border-color: var(--lite-accent); }
.lite-app button:disabled { opacity: 0.5; cursor: default; }
.lite-game-state { position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; width: calc(320px * var(--k)); height: calc(240px * var(--k)); display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; pointer-events: none; color: #fff; font: 600 15px var(--sans-serif, system-ui); letter-spacing: 0.02em; background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.45); border-radius: 4px; }
.lite-game-state[hidden] { display: none; }
.lite-tests-toggle { padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid var(--lite-accent); border-radius: 6px; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--lite-accent) 10%, transparent); max-width: 260px; }
.lite-tests-toggle label { font-weight: 600; }
.lite-tests .lite-summary .lite-badge { margin-right: 2px; }
.lite-bench td:first-child { color: var(--lite-muted-text); }
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .lp2-pane .lopecode-visualizer { padding: 0 16px; box-sizing: border-box; }
  .lp2-pane .observablehq > div > table { display: block; overflow-x: auto; max-width: 100%; }
  .lp2-pane .observablehq pre { margin-left: 0; margin-right: 0; }
}
.lp2-pane .observablehq pre, .lp2-pane .observablehq--inspect { overflow-x: auto; max-width: 100%; }
.lp2-pane .observablehq--inspect { display: block; }
</style>`
// %%
viewof tutorial_module = thisModule()
// %%
test_ss_roundtrip = {
  tests_on;
  const n = ss_roundtrip(tutorial_module);
  if (n < 33) throw new Error(`only ${n} SoundScript variables found`);
  return `${n} variables recompile to the same text and inputs`;
}
// %%
test_ss_module_checks = {
  tests_on;
  const { result } = ss_check(tutorial_module);
  const got = Object.fromEntries(result.cells.map((c) => [c.name, c.status]));
  const want = { width: "ok", height: "ok", area: "ok", aspect: "ok", scale: "ok", half: "ok", test_half: "ok", samples: "ok", histogram: "ok", grown: "ok", twoNames: "ok", count: "ok", increment: "ok", clear: "ok", saved: "ok", save: "ok", app: "ok", measure: "ok", word: "ok", wordSize: "ok", measureAsked: "ok", measureWord: "ok", measured: "ok", wordSizes: "ok", storeSize: "ok", onSize: "ok", measureView: "ok", n: "ok", inc: "ok", setN: "ok", Badge: "ok", NotElem: "ok", bad_attr_type: "rejected", bad_unknown_attr: "rejected", bad_unknown_element: "rejected", bad_handler_not_action: "rejected", bad_handler_wrong_args: "rejected", bad_component_props: "rejected", bad_component_missing_prop: "rejected", bad_component_not_elem: "rejected", bad_mut_in_jsx: "rejected", bad_child_object: "rejected", ok_all: "ok", shopTotal: "ok", cheaper: "ok", shopView: "ok" };
  if (JSON.stringify(got) !== JSON.stringify(want)) throw new Error(`verdicts ${JSON.stringify(got)}`);
  if (result.module.length) throw new Error(`module errors ${JSON.stringify(result.module)}`);
  return `${result.cells.length} cells: the ten bad_* rejected, the rest ok`;
}
